issues.body:
If we are going to ship experimental versions of MoQ (which we should), those probably should be interoperable on a handshake level (meaning that a server can accept two clients speaking different iterations of MoQ).  We currently have the following format in the draft:

```
SETUP Parameter {
  Parameter Key (i),
  Parameter Value Length (i),
  Parameter Value (..),
}

Client SETUP Message Payload {
  Number of Supported Versions (i),
  Supported Version (i) ...,
  SETUP Parameters (..) ...,
}

Server SETUP Message Payload {
  Selected Version (i),
  SETUP Parameters (..) ...,
}
```

Astute reader will notice that this negotiation mechanism is similar to [a certain other IETF protocol](https://tlswg.org/tls13-spec/draft-ietf-tls-rfc8446bis.html#name-supported-versions).

The question I want to explicitly ask here is: are we okay with this format?  We obviously can change it later down the line, but that would be tricky since we'd create an incompatibility (and we also don't have the Alt-Svc escape hatch that QUIC used).

