@suhasHere : Can you give some example end-to-scenarios involving PUBLISH_REQUEST?

I see that PUBLISH_REQUEST contains a subset of the contents of the catalog (currently only track name) and it is efficiently relay readable.  It's therefore possible for any relay to issue a PUBLISH_OK before any consuming endpoint with catalog access could issue a SUBSCRIBE.  Is that the intent?

I think @kixelated's question is how would a generic relay decide which tracks to allow when all it knows is their name?

@suhasHere the distinction is very important.

If the encoder creates the catalog, then the contribution and distribution functionality the same. The encoder/transcoder advertises the list of available tracks, and the origin/player chooses which ones to subscribe to. There's a very clear set of responsibilities for the producer and consumer regardless of the endpoint.

If the origin creates the catalog instead, then we need separate PUBLISH/SUBSCRIBE messages like #123 has proposed. This actually forks the protocol, based on if the endpoint is before or after the origin. A MoQ library on the contribution side (ex. OBS -> ingest) does not work the same way on the distribution side (ie. CDN -> VLC).

I started migrating to the current draft but I gave up.

With #258 every subscription is stored in a lookup table. When I receive an `OBJECT` or `SUBSCRIBE_*` message, I use a lookup table to fetch the current state. This is possible because `SUBSCRIBE` contains the `track_id`:

```rust
struct Publisher {
  // maps from track_id to subscription
  subscriptions: HashMap<VarInt, track::Publisher>,
}
```

I started implementing the current draft where `SUBSCRIBE_OK` contains `track_id`, and this is what I think is needed:
```rust
struct Publisher {
  // maps from (namespace, name) to subscription
  subscriptions: HashMap<(String, String), track::Publisher>,

  // maps from track_id to (namespace, name) after receiving SUBSCRIBE_OK
  aliases: HashMap<VarInt, Deferred<(String, String)>>,
}
```

`Deferred` doesn't exist in Rust but my point is that it's an asynchronous lookup table.  I need to check if there's an entry in `aliases` or create one, which will be resolved when/if `SUBSCRIBE_OK` arrives. My `OBJECT` decoder will block until `SUBSCRIBE_OK` arrives, which might actually never happen if an attacker sends OBJECTs with random `track_id`s.

It's just a pain in the butt. The performance arguments are moot if you need to perform a 2nd lookup for each OBJECT. I think you can avoid it with separate lookup tables, but that adds more complexity. The complexity is completely avoided if the subscriber chooses the `track_id`.

#305 replaces `(String, String)` with `VarInt` but it's otherwise the same problem.

@afrind agreed.  Even for cases where they are not end to end encrypted, the roles does need to talk about it. Relay Role for example are not expected to access/modify raw media or cannot access the raw media. 
I am happy to amend the definitions in the arch spec to reflect non-e2ee cases

Driving the terminology based on the roles will help us to reason about the system as we start add more details.

