@vasilvv : with ALIAS_ACK in the proposal, no buffering is ever required of the subscriber.  However, publishers are not prohibited from sending objects with the Alias before ALIAS_ACK either, which can lead to drops.  This is a potential interoperability problem, when a risk-taking publisher meets a non-buffering subscriber, though FETCH is always there to get stuff you may have lost.  In practice, I would probably write my publishers/relays to use the Client-Chosen ID from SUBSCRIBE until ALIAS_ACK, but in some use-cases and systems, particularly where the Publisher's ID is known in advance by the subscriber, there's no risk.

I believe allowing a publisher-chosen ID is critical not so much for SUBSCRIBE but for PUBLISH, and once we have that mechanism, adding to SUBSCRIBE is more symmetric.

@ianswett @fluffy absorbed all the points of my proposal into their PUBLISH proposal except ALIAS_ACK.  The details are still being sketched out, though a PUBLISH_OK message would serve the same purpose of ALIAS_ACK, at the cost of 1-RTT before being able to publish objects.  They also advocated that subscribers should just buffer.  

My concern with buffering is the non-determinism introduced by implementations having to have limits - how much and how long to buffer are sufficient to know that I'm not dropping something?  Perhaps another approach is a SETUP param limiting the amount of data that can be sent before PUBLISH_OK/ALIAS_ACK.  If the publisher hits the limit, it's similar to hitting a flow-control limit - the publisher is blocked on the subscription and it starts to count against DELIVERY_TIMEOUT, if any.

+1 on removing ASC for Subscribe/SubscribeOk and leaving it for Fetch if it addresses real use-cases or revisiting it later.

> In simulations I ran for live use cases, DESC without timeouts was clearly advantageous against ASC without timeouts, so there's that.

@vasilvv my  tests with jitter buffer of size 5 seconds, **ASC with timeout** had better experiences especially when link was temporarily congested.  I haven't tried without timeouts though. Can you share more about your experiments. Thanks

@vasilvv ,

> I don't believe this is true: groups are delivered independently, and the network provides no ordering guarantees.

we are agreeing on the same thing. Interconnecting relays via the network does not provide ordering… hence it’s the order received to order transmitted… based on the original publish transmission (aka order). In other words, as received will be delivered in that order. The relays should not be changing order to ensure asc or desc for live/real time subscribes. Again, fetch is an exception. Just my two cents…

@vasilvv and I discussed this after it was discussed at the virtual interim and the case where the two are significantly different is when the publisher is delivering a group and data from the next group begins arriving.  If for example the first Object in the next group is an I-frame, so is both critical to playback and larger than other Objects, then possibly there are cases when starting to deliver the I-frame is better, because otherwise the full I-frame won't arrive in time?  But that would only occur when you're running very close to fully utilizing bandwidth AND there are no lower-priority Objects (ie: temporal scalability) that can be dropped as the connection becomes bandwidth constrained.

I agree that without Delivery Timeout, there are cases descending is likely better, but we do have Delivery Timeout, so we should evaluate it with that.

My intuition is that real-world applications will always use ascending, but if everyone has already implemented descending and it's not causing undue complexity, I'm ok with leaving it in for now.

I'm disinclined to keep this issue tracking using a bidirectional stream for FETCH.  It solves some problems (guaranteed return stream) but creates others (see https://github.com/moq-wg/moq-transport/issues/632#issuecomment-2605450825, and also would break Joining FETCH).

I think it's sufficient to add editorial text indicating that receivers need to have a timeout for the data stream for a FETCH to arrive, and indicate that RESET_STREAM_AT offers advantages to the receiver in this regard.

@vasilvv or @ianswett if you want to change fetch to a bidi stream, please open a separate issue.

